  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You are given an array AAA of length NNN, and a positive integer KKK.\n\nIt is guaranteed that 1≤Ai≤K1 \\leq A_i \\leq K1≤Ai​≤K for every index iii from 111 to NNN.\n\nYou can do the following **at most once**:\n\n\nChoose an index iii (1≤i≤N1 \\leq i \\leq N1≤i≤N) and a value xxx (1≤x≤K1 \\leq x \\leq K1≤x≤K).\nThen, set Ai:=xA_i := xAi​:=x.\nFind the **maximum** possible value of the sum of adjacent differences of AAA after performing this operation at most once.\n\nThat is, maximize the quantity\n\n∑i=1N−1∣Ai−Ai+1∣\\sum_{i=1}^{N-1} |A_i - A_{i+1}|i=1∑N−1​∣Ai​−Ai+1​∣\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of two lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and KKK — the length of the array and the maximum allowed integer KKK, respectively.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, the elements of array AAA.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: the maximum possible sum of adjacent differences of AAA after replacing at most one element.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\leq T \\leq 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤10001 \\leq N \\leq 10001≤N≤1000",
      "1≤K≤2⋅1061 \\leq K \\leq 2\\cdot 10^61≤K≤2⋅106",
      "1≤Ai≤K1 \\leq A_i \\leq K1≤Ai​≤K",
      "The sum of NNN across all tests won't exceed 100010001000."
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "3\n2 5\n1 5\n3 8\n7 2 7\n5 20\n18 3 1 4 19",
        "output": "4\n12\n63",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: It's best to leave the array unchanged, giving us a difference of ∣1−5∣=4|1-5| = 4∣1−5∣=4.\nTest case 222: It's optimal to set A2:=1A_2 := 1A2​:=1, giving us the array [7,1,7][7, 1, 7][7,1,7]. The sum of adjacent differences is 6+6=126 + 6 = 126+6=12.\nTest case 333: It's optimal to set A3:=20A_3 := 20A3​:=20, to obtain [18,3,20,4,19][18, 3, 20, 4, 19][18,3,20,4,19]. The sum of adjacent differences is 636363."
      }
    ]
  },
